                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a696dfe2-41ce-3fcf-9d05-97f58bd748c2",
                        "entry_id": "b7d58fed-a734-342a-a91f-79254bfdee39",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Monomorphic."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f90cf85b-b39c-31c3-8559-067c476c7d19",
                        "entry_id": "b7d58fed-a734-342a-a91f-79254bfdee39",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Of a morphism: that it is a monomorphism."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "6b885a92-d0c6-4f89-bc5a-aa0c6388c0b9",
                                "sense_id": "f90cf85b-b39c-31c3-8559-067c476c7d19",
                                "text": "If a composition (of morphisms) is monic then, by theorem, the pre-composite of it is monic as well."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "503e4767-5c8d-44a1-a059-ef949d2c66ca",
                                "sense_id": "f90cf85b-b39c-31c3-8559-067c476c7d19",
                                "text": "It is often convenient to view a monic arrow i#58;A#92;rightarrowtailC, defined in Chapter 1, as showing that A is a copy of a part of C, and that i maps the copy on to that part. For example, in Set, the monic arrows are the one-to-one functions and, clearly, if i is one-to-one then A is a copy of a subset of C, namely of the image of i."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
